<!-- HEADER -->
<div class="report-header">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Biomedical Research Landscape 2024–2026</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">Publication Trends, Topic Dynamics, and Open Access Patterns — A Comprehensive Trend Analysis of 4,886 Biomedical Research Abstracts Indexed in PubMed</div>
    <div class="meta">Report Date: April 21, 2026 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Data Source: NCBI PubMed (Jan 2024 – Mar 2026) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Audience: Research Policy Analysts &amp; Funding Strategists</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- KPI STRIP -->
<div class="container">
  <div class="kpi-strip">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">4,886</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Publications</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">2024: 3,924 · 2025: 737 · 2026: 225</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">64.9%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Overall Open Access Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">2024: 64.1% · 2025: 74.1% · 2026: 53.8%</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">6.0</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Median Authors per Paper</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">Stable across all three years</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">51.5%</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Top 10 Countries Share</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">China (21.0%) leads; USA (10.7%) second</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-value">COVID-19</div>
      <div class="kpi-label">Top Major MeSH Topic</div>
      <div class="kpi-detail">43 publications; persistent across all years</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1: Publication Volume -->
<div class="container">
<div class="section" id="publication-volume-trends">
  <h2>1. Publication Volume &amp; Temporal Distribution</h2>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">The dataset captures <strong>4,886 biomedical abstracts</strong> spanning January 2024 through March 2026. The pronounced concentration in 2024 (80.3% of records) reflects PubMed's indexing pipeline — older publications accumulate more indexed entries over time. This temporal skew is a known artifact rather than a data quality issue, and all year-over-year comparisons below use normalized rates rather than raw counts.</p>

  <h3>Yearly Breakdown</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Publications</th><th>Share of Total</th><th>Open Access Rate</th><th>Median Authors</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>2024</strong></td><td>3,924</td><td>80.3%</td><td>64.1%</td><td>6.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>2025</strong></td><td>737</td><td>15.1%</td><td>74.1%</td><td>6.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>2026</strong></td><td>225</td><td>4.6%</td><td>53.8%</td><td>6.0</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="caveat">Note: 754 records (15.4%) have unknown publication months and are excluded from monthly trend charts but included in yearly aggregates. The 2026 data covers only Q1 (Jan–Mar) and may understate OA rates due to PMC embargo delays.</p>

  <h3>Monthly Publication Volume</h3>
  <div class="chart-container" style="max-height:400px">
    <canvas id="monthlyVolumeChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p><strong>Key Observation:</strong> January 2024 alone accounts for 1,665 publications (34% of the dataset), reflecting a heavy concentration of indexed records at the start of the observation window. From mid-2024 onward, monthly volumes stabilize at 30–100 records. The 2025 data shows a notably even distribution (26–94 per month), suggesting a more current indexing cadence. Early 2026 volumes (51–98 per month) show a rising trajectory consistent with ongoing PubMed processing.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Seasonality Patterns</h3>
  <p>Within 2024, a clear front-loading pattern is visible: Q1 accounts for 62% of that year's records. In 2025, the distribution is much flatter, with a modest uptick in December (94 records). These patterns reflect indexing timing rather than true publication seasonality, and should be interpreted accordingly when projecting future volumes.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2: Geographic Landscape -->
<div class="section" id="geographic-landscape">
  <h2>2. Geographic Landscape of Biomedical Research</h2>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">First-author country affiliations reveal a research landscape dominated by China and the United States, together accounting for nearly a third of all publications. The top 10 countries contribute 51.5% of all indexed records.</p>

  <h3>Top 10 Contributing Countries</h3>
  <div class="chart-row">
    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="countryBarChart"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Publications</th><th>Share</th><th>OA Rate</th><th>Median Authors</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>China</strong></td><td>1,028</td><td>21.0%</td><td>57.5%</td><td>7.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>USA</strong></td><td>521</td><td>10.7%</td><td>76.6%</td><td>6.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>India</strong></td><td>196</td><td>4.0%</td><td>68.9%</td><td>5.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Japan</strong></td><td>137</td><td>2.8%</td><td>77.4%</td><td>6.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Iran</strong></td><td>107</td><td>2.2%</td><td>86.9%</td><td>5.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Canada</strong></td><td>107</td><td>2.2%</td><td>72.9%</td><td>5.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Germany</strong></td><td>98</td><td>2.0%</td><td>73.5%</td><td>6.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Italy</strong></td><td>89</td><td>1.8%</td><td>68.5%</td><td>8.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>South Korea</strong></td><td>87</td><td>1.8%</td><td>71.3%</td><td>5.0</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>UK</strong></td><td>78</td><td>1.6%</td><td>61.5%</td><td>6.0</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="caveat">China figures combine "China" and "PR China" designations. USA combines "USA" and "United States." 144 records (2.9%) have unknown country affiliations.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Geographic Share Shifts (2024 → 2025)</h3>
  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p><strong>India's Rising Share:</strong> India's contribution doubled from 3.3% of publications in 2024 to 8.0% in 2025, the most notable geographic shift in the dataset. Iran similarly rose from 2.0% to 3.7%. Meanwhile, China's share decreased from 22.6% to 13.2% in 2025 before rebounding to 20.4% in early 2026. The USA maintained a relatively stable share (10.9% → 9.6%).</p>
  </div>
  <p>Italy's share halved from 2.1% (2024) to 0.9% (2025), while the UK declined from 1.8% to 0.7%. These shifts may reflect differences in PubMed indexing timelines across journals with varying geographic footprints rather than true declines in research output.</p>

  <h3>Collaboration Intensity by Country</h3>
  <p>Italy stands out with the highest median author count (8.0), suggesting larger, more collaborative research teams. China follows with a median of 7.0 authors per paper. India, Iran, Canada, and South Korea tend toward smaller teams (median 5.0 authors), which may reflect different research funding structures and team composition norms.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3: Topics & MeSH -->
<div class="section" id="topic-and-mesh-analysis">
  <h2>3. Research Topic &amp; MeSH Keyword Analysis</h2>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">Of the 4,886 records, 2,583 (52.9%) have assigned MeSH major topics — a gap that reflects the delay inherent in NLM's indexing process, particularly for newer publications. Keyword analysis draws on 2,583 topic-tagged records and a broader set with semicolon-delimited keyword fields.</p>

  <h3>Most Prevalent Major Topics</h3>
  <div class="chart-container" style="max-height:400px">
    <canvas id="topTopicsChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Major Topic</th><th>Publications</th><th>Share of Indexed</th><th>Trend</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>COVID-19</strong></td><td>43</td><td>1.66%</td><td><span class="badge badge-neutral">Persistent</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Diabetes Mellitus, Type 2</strong></td><td>24</td><td>0.93%</td><td><span class="badge badge-neutral">Concentrated 2024</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Breast Neoplasms</strong></td><td>17</td><td>0.66%</td><td><span class="badge badge-neutral">Stable</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Gastrointestinal Microbiome</strong></td><td>16</td><td>0.62%</td><td><span class="badge badge-up">Growing</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Alzheimer Disease</strong></td><td>15</td><td>0.58%</td><td><span class="badge badge-up">Growing</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Carcinoma, Hepatocellular</strong></td><td>11</td><td>0.43%</td><td><span class="badge badge-neutral">Stable</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Quality of Life</strong></td><td>11</td><td>0.43%</td><td><span class="badge badge-neutral">Stable</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>MicroRNAs</strong></td><td>11</td><td>0.43%</td><td><span class="badge badge-up">Growing</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Coleoptera</strong></td><td>10</td><td>0.39%</td><td><span class="badge badge-up">Growing</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Anti-Bacterial Agents</strong></td><td>10</td><td>0.39%</td><td><span class="badge badge-neutral">Stable</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Most Frequent MeSH Keywords (Across All Indexed Records)</h3>
  <p>Beyond demographics tags (Humans, Female, Male, Animals), the most substantive keyword themes include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Methodology:</strong> Retrospective Studies (190), Cross-Sectional Studies (124), Prospective Studies (68)</li>
    <li><strong>Disease Focus:</strong> COVID-19 (88), Neoplasms (64), Anti-Bacterial Agents (73)</li>
    <li><strong>Population:</strong> Child (155), Adolescent (104), Pregnancy (76)</li>
    <li><strong>Analytical:</strong> Risk Factors (69), Treatment Outcome (71), Algorithms (47)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Emerging Topic Dynamics</h3>
  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p><strong>Emerging Signals:</strong> When comparing topic share between 2024 and 2025, <strong>Coleoptera</strong> (beetle taxonomy) shows the sharpest proportional growth, rising from 0.30% to 1.44% of indexed records — a +1.14 percentage point shift, driven by a cluster of biodiversity studies. <strong>COVID-19</strong> research, while declining in absolute volume, maintained a growing share of indexed records (1.59% → 2.40%), indicating its continued dominance even as the pandemic recedes. <strong>Alzheimer Disease</strong> shows renewed momentum with 2 publications in July 2025 after a quiet mid-2024 period.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="caveat">Topic growth rates should be interpreted with caution given the small sample sizes in 2025–2026 and the 47.1% of records lacking MeSH topic assignments.</p>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4: Open Access -->
