<!-- SECTION 6: Key Takeaways -->
<div class="section" id="key-takeaways">
  <h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</h2>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">The following findings carry the most direct implications for research policy, funding prioritization, and institutional strategy.</p>

  <div class="takeaway-grid">
    <div class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="number">1</div>
      <h4>Open Access Momentum Is Real — But Uneven</h4>
      <p>The OA rate rose from 64.1% (2024) to 74.1% (2025), a 10 percentage point jump. However, this varies sharply by geography: Iran (86.9%) and Japan (77.4%) lead, while China — the largest contributor — trails at 57.5%. <strong>Implication:</strong> Funding bodies targeting OA compliance should consider country-specific support mechanisms, particularly for Chinese-affiliated researchers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="number">2</div>
      <h4>China Dominates Volume; India Is the Fastest Riser</h4>
      <p>China accounts for 21.0% of publications, more than double the USA's share. India's contribution doubled from 3.3% to 8.0% between 2024 and 2025. <strong>Implication:</strong> Collaborative funding programs with Indian institutions may yield outsized returns as that research ecosystem scales rapidly.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="number">3</div>
      <h4>COVID-19 Research Persists — But the Field Is Shifting</h4>
      <p>COVID-19 remains the single most-indexed major topic (43 records), but is joined by rising interest in the gut microbiome, Alzheimer disease, and microRNA biology. <strong>Implication:</strong> Strategic rebalancing of pandemic-era funding toward neurodegenerative disease and microbiome research aligns with observable publication momentum.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="number">4</div>
      <h4>Mega-Journals and OA Platforms Are Reshaping Publishing</h4>
      <p>Scientific Reports, Heliyon, and Cureus top the journal rankings. The long tail is extreme: no single journal exceeds 1.7% of output. <strong>Implication:</strong> Institutional journal-level evaluation metrics are becoming less meaningful as publishing disperses across a broadening OA landscape.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="number">5</div>
      <h4>Collaboration Size Is Stable and Substantial</h4>
      <p>The median of 6 authors per paper held constant across 2024–2026, with 83% of publications involving 4+ authors. Italy (median 8) and China (median 7) lead in team size. <strong>Implication:</strong> Funding models should continue to support multi-investigator grants; solo research is vanishingly rare in biomedical science (1.6%).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway-item">
      <div class="number">6</div>
      <h4>MeSH Indexing Lag Limits Real-Time Topic Intelligence</h4>
      <p>47.1% of records lack major topic assignments, concentrated in newer publications. This limits the utility of MeSH-based trend analysis for the most recent periods. <strong>Implication:</strong> Organizations relying on MeSH for strategic planning should supplement with NLP-based topic extraction on raw abstracts to capture emerging trends earlier.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:24px;">
    <p><strong>Data Limitations:</strong> This analysis is based on a curated sample of 4,886 records from a larger universe of 126,832 abstracts. The temporal distribution is heavily skewed toward 2024 due to PubMed indexing lag. All percentages and trends should be understood within this context. Small sample sizes in 2025–2026 limit the statistical power of year-over-year comparisons. The Q1 2026 open-access dip is most likely an artifact of PMC embargo timing rather than a reversal in OA adoption.</p>
  </div>
</div>

